1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,290
Narrator: The Law Behind the Deal.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:06,678
Narrator: Episode one. Who makes the rules?

3
00:00:12,778 --> 00:00:16,598
Kito: The supplier broke our deal! I want them punished, and I want our money back!

4
00:00:16,848 --> 00:00:19,804
Nia: Hmm. Those might be two different legal questions.

5
00:00:20,754 --> 00:00:24,541
Professor Lex: Exactly. First, identify the type of problem.

6
00:00:24,691 --> 00:00:32,372
Professor Lex: A civil dispute is about a remedy for a private wrong. A criminal case is a prosecution for an offence.

7
00:00:36,872 --> 00:00:44,987
Professor Lex: A civil claim commonly seeks compensation or another remedy. The usual standard is the balance of probabilities.

8
00:00:45,337 --> 00:00:52,427
Professor Lex: A criminal prosecution concerns an offence, and uses the higher standard: beyond reasonable doubt.

9
00:00:52,777 --> 00:00:57,248
Nia: So a broken contract does not automatically mean someone goes to prison.

10
00:00:57,348 --> 00:01:01,583
Professor Lex: Correct. Always classify the facts before choosing the rule.

11
00:01:05,483 --> 00:01:07,949
Professor Lex: The legislature makes laws.

12
00:01:07,999 --> 00:01:10,901
Professor Lex: The executive puts them into action.

13
00:01:10,951 --> 00:01:15,331
Professor Lex: And the judiciary resolves disputes, and interprets the law.

14
00:01:15,581 --> 00:01:19,280
Kito: So the judge does not usually write every new law from scratch?

15
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:26,171
Professor Lex: Right. But judicial decisions can shape legal principles, especially in common-law systems.

16
00:01:32,471 --> 00:01:38,485
Professor Lex: The ratio decidendi is the legal reason necessary for the earlier decision.

17
00:01:38,585 --> 00:01:42,175
Professor Lex: Obiter dicta are additional observations.

18
00:01:42,425 --> 00:01:50,415
Professor Lex: A precedent may bind a later court when it comes from a superior court, and the material facts are sufficiently similar.

19
00:01:51,565 --> 00:01:54,721
Professor Lex: Reverse: the same case goes up on appeal.

20
00:01:54,771 --> 00:01:59,111
Professor Lex: Overrule: a later case changes the earlier legal rule.

21
00:01:59,161 --> 00:02:02,172
Professor Lex: Distinguish: the important facts differ.

22
00:02:02,522 --> 00:02:11,166
Professor Lex: Do not mix these up. Read the facts, identify the court hierarchy, and ask what part of the earlier judgment was necessary.

23
00:02:14,666 --> 00:02:18,177
Kito: They never delivered our custom machine. Fine, just pay us cash!

24
00:02:18,327 --> 00:02:22,508
Nia: But Kito, that machine is one of a kind. Cash can't buy another one.

25
00:02:22,758 --> 00:02:27,820
Professor Lex: Equitable remedies developed because damages are not always adequate.

26
00:02:28,070 --> 00:02:29,922
Professor Lex: Specific performance.

27
00:02:29,922 --> 00:02:31,356
Professor Lex: An injunction.

28
00:02:31,356 --> 00:02:32,736
Professor Lex: Rescission.

29
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:34,381
Professor Lex: Rectification.

30
00:02:34,631 --> 00:02:39,832
Professor Lex: These remedies are discretionary, and depend on the legal conditions.

31
00:02:39,982 --> 00:02:44,821
Nia: So I must not assume every claimant automatically gets the remedy they request.

32
00:02:48,921 --> 00:02:54,998
Professor Lex: International trade needs cooperation, because different countries may have different laws.

33
00:02:55,148 --> 00:03:02,608
Professor Lex: Treaties and conventions can create shared rules. Model laws offer a template for countries to adopt.

34
00:03:02,758 --> 00:03:06,546
Professor Lex: UNCITRAL develops harmonised commercial-law texts.

35
00:03:06,596 --> 00:03:10,966
Professor Lex: The WTO provides a framework for trade cooperation.

36
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:19,181
Professor Lex: And the ICC develops commercial rules, including Incoterms, and supports commercial dispute resolution.

37
00:03:20,231 --> 00:03:24,529
Captain Cargo: Ahoy! And who decides whether our case goes to court, or arbitration?

38
00:03:24,629 --> 00:03:28,162
Professor Lex: That depends on the agreement, and the applicable law.

39
00:03:31,862 --> 00:03:35,725
Professor Lex: Court adjudication is formal, and usually public.

40
00:03:35,975 --> 00:03:47,717
Professor Lex: Arbitration may offer privacy, flexibility and specialist expertise. But it can still be expensive, and its award can only be challenged on limited grounds.

41
00:03:47,967 --> 00:03:49,149
Nia: And mediation?

42
00:03:49,299 --> 00:03:56,459
Professor Lex: A neutral mediator helps the parties negotiate. The mediator does not normally impose a decision.

43
00:03:57,609 --> 00:04:04,138
The Auditor: Quick check. Which mechanism usually has a third party impose a binding decision, under the parties' agreement?

44
00:04:08,188 --> 00:04:09,629
The Auditor: Arbitration.

45
00:04:13,379 --> 00:04:14,972
Nia: Civil versus criminal.

46
00:04:15,022 --> 00:04:17,516
Nia: Legislature, executive, judiciary.

47
00:04:17,566 --> 00:04:19,337
Nia: Ratio versus obiter.

48
00:04:19,387 --> 00:04:21,900
Nia: Damages versus equitable remedies.

49
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:23,695
Nia: Court versus ADR.

50
00:04:23,945 --> 00:04:33,465
Professor Lex: Excellent. Next time, SafariTech's shipment will cross an ocean, and the contract will decide who pays, who delivers, and when risk moves.

51
00:04:34,315 --> 00:04:36,702
Captain Cargo: I hope someone chose the right Incoterm!

52
00:04:37,252 --> 00:04:41,328
Narrator: Next time: Episode two. The Contract Across the Ocean.

